{
  "schema": "vajra.astrology.golden_charts/v1",
  "description": "Immutable reference charts for core.astrology.AstrologicalCalculator. The expected values are INDEPENDENT of the project: they were produced by astropy's BUILTIN ERFA ephemeris, using a derivation that shares no code with pyswisseph. Storing project-derived values here would make every assertion a tautology.",
  "provenance": {
    "audit_of_record": "docs/notes/w0-astrology-audit-accuracy.md",
    "method": "Apparent geocentric position from astropy (builtin ERFA; solar_system_ephemeris not set to any downloadable set, iers.conf.auto_download=False), then transform_to(FK5(equinox=t)) to precess to the mean equinox OF DATE, then transform_to(GeocentricTrueEcliptic(equinox=t)) to obtain apparent geocentric ecliptic longitude of date - which is the quantity swe.calc_ut returns.",
    "time_scale": "Instants are UTC. The project passes UT to swe.calc_ut/swe.houses, which apply Delta-T internally, so no manual Delta-T is applied anywhere in this fixture. C1 is the case where the distinction matters: J2000.0 is 12:00:00 TT, which is 11:58:55.8166 UT.",
    "astropy_version": "8.0.1",
    "erfa_version": "2.0.1.5",
    "pyswisseph_version": "2.10.03",
    "ephemeris": "builtin (ERFA/VSOP87/PLAN94). No JPL kernel is present offline.",
    "asc_mc_derivation": "MC = the ecliptic point whose right ascension of date equals the local apparent sidereal time; RA(lambda) = atan2(sin(lambda) cos(eps_true), cos(lambda)) with eps_true = erfa.obl06(TT) + erfa.nut06a(TT) nutation in obliquity. Two solutions exist (MC and IC); the higher-altitude one is by definition the culminating point. ASC = the ecliptic point on the horizon whose azimuth lies in the eastern half (0,180), located by scanning astropy AltAz. Selecting by azimuth is essential: at high latitude 'altitude rises with longitude' selects the DESCENDANT.",
    "tolerances_arcsec": {
      "sun": 5,
      "moon": 30,
      "mercury": 90,
      "venus": 90,
      "mars": 90,
      "jupiter": 90,
      "saturn": 90,
      "uranus": 90,
      "neptune": 90,
      "pluto": 90,
      "ASC": 40,
      "MC": 40
    },
    "tolerance_rationale": "Sun <= 5: both series are VSOP87-grade and Delta-T/aberration contribute well under 1\", so 5\" leaves headroom without being vacuous. Moon <= 30: the Moon is the hardest body and the two implementations truncate the lunar theory differently. Planets <= 90: Jupiter and Saturn reached 26-36\" in W0-B, so 90\" accommodates the outer-planet term truncation. ASC/MC <= 40: two separate nutation and sidereal-time chains; W0-B measured <= 21\".",
    "known_limits": [
      "Pluto is marked unverifiable: astropy builtin has no Pluto and no JPL kernel is available offline.",
      "This is a CONSISTENCY cross-check between two independent analytic theories, not a proof of absolute accuracy. A systematic error shared by both would be invisible to it.",
      "Lunar nodes have no independent offline reference and are therefore not given expected values; they are covered by self-consistency assertions instead."
    ]
  },
  "corrections": {
    "w0b_c1_to_c4_instant_offset": "The W0-B harness converted its four astropy-derived instants with datetime.fromisoformat(...).astimezone(UTC) on a NAIVE datetime, so Python applied the machine's local UTC offset ON EACH DATE - a shift that is DST-dependent, not a constant: C1 (2000-01-01, America/Los_Angeles PST, -08:00) was shifted +8 hours, while C2..C4 (March/April/June 2024, PDT, -07:00) were shifted +7 hours. The values stored in that note are mutually consistent (project and independent received the same wrong instant) so its deltas stand, but its C1..C4 INSTANTS are later than the labels it printed, by +8 h for C1 and +7 h for C2..C4. This fixture recomputes C1..C4 at the correct instants. C5..C10 were built with an explicit tzinfo=UTC in W0-B and are unaffected - see note_crosscheck.",
